2†. A net for binding up or confining the hair.

{{smaller block|Her golden lockes she roundly did uptye
In breaded ''tramels''. {{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. ii. 15.}}}}

3. A shackle; specifically, a kind of shackle used for regulating the motions of a horse, and making him amble.—4. Whatever hinders activity, freedom, or progress; an impediment.

{{smaller block|Prose . . . is loose, easy, and free from ''trammels''.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Pref. to Poetical Dict.}}}}

{{smaller block|It is impossible not to be struck with his [William IV.'s] extreme good-nature and simplicity, which he cannot or will not exchange for the dignity of his new situation and the ''trammels'' of etiquette.
{{right|''Greville'', Memoirs, July 24, 1830.}}}}

5. An implement hung in a fireplace to support pots and other culinary vessels. {{smaller|Trammels are hung from the back-bar or from a crane; they are often so constructed in two parts that they can be lengthened and shortened.}}

{{smaller block|Our own warm hearth seemed blazing free,
The crane and pendent ''trammels'' showed,
The Turks' heads on the andirons glowed.
{{right|''Whittier'', Snow-Bound.}}}}

6. An instrument for drawing ellipses, used by joiners and other artificers; an ellipsograph. {{smaller|One part consists of}}

